  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Let $X$  be the set of all non-negative integer powers of $2$ and let $Y$  be the set of all non-negative integer powers of $3.$  What is $X-Y?$",
  "worked_solution": "The powers of $2$ that are greater or equal to $1$ are\n\n$$\nX={1,2,4,8,16,32,\u2026}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, the powers of $3$ that are greater or equal to $1$ are\n\n$$\nY={1,3,9,27,81,\u2026}.\n$$\n\nNow, $X-Y$ is the *difference* of $X$ and $Y,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in $X$ but *not* in $Y.$\n\nThe only element of $X$ that are also in $Y$ is $1.$ So, we must remove this element from $X.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X-Y & ={1,2,4,8,16,32,\u2026}-{1,3,9,27,81,\u2026} \\\\  & ={2,4,8,16,32,\u2026}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
